                      {
                        "id": "claude-event-0",
                        "phase": "item.completed",
                        "status": "completed",
                        "title": "item.completed",
                        "detail": "I'll write the optimized run.py with a different direction than candidate-01 (which used sequential in-place ops). This time I'll use a pre-allocated output buffer and a single fused `torch.addcmul`-style expression, avoiding the intermediate allocation chain entirely.",
                        "time": "刚刚"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "claude-event-1",
                        "phase": "item.completed",
                        "status": "completed",
                        "title": "item.completed",
                        "detail": "{\"schemaVersion\":\"operator-studio.agent-result/v1\",\"summary\":\"Wrote a new run.py implementing a different optimization direction than candidate-01. Instead of an in-place op chain, run(inputs) now uses (a) a fully zero-allocation path where every elementwise op writes into a preallocated output buffer via out=, and (b) a CUDA Graph capture/replay of the whole 4-kernel chain, since a 32x256 (8192-element) elementwise chain is purely launch-overhead bound. Replay is guarded by an input-pointer equality check and capture is wrapped in try/except with a global disable flag and per-shape attempt cap, so any mismatch or capture failure falls back to the correct zero-allocation eager path. get_inputs/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs/reference are byte-identical to the baseline; only run(inputs) and its private helpers changed.\",\"diagnosis\":{\"summary\":\"Baseline is 14.336us for 8192 fp32 elements. Arithmetic itself is sub-microsecond; the measured latency is dominated by per-call allocation (value.clone() plus 3 intermediate temporaries) and by 4 separate kernel launches at ~2-3us each. candidate-01 removed the allocation but kept 4 eager launches, landing at 11.168us — confirming launch overhead, not memory traffic, is the residual bottleneck.\",\"bottlenecks\":[\"Per-call caching-allocator round-trips for the clone and intermediate temporaries\",\"Four separate eager kernel launches on a tensor small enough that each launch costs more than the kernel itself\",\"Python-level dispatch overhead of chained tensor ops\"]},\"candidates\":[{\"id\":\"candidate-01\",\"title\":\"Zero-allocation fused chain replayed via CUDA Graph\",\"hypothesis\":\"For an 8192-element fp32 elementwise chain the runtime is launch-bound, not compute-bound. Removing all allocations via out= plus collapsing the 4 launches into one graph replay should cut p50 substantially below the 11.168us reached by the in-place-chain approach.\",\"change\":\"run(inputs) computes relu(x*1.25 + y*0.75 - 0.125) by (1) capturing the four ops (torch.mul(...,out=), add_(y, alpha=0.75), sub_(0.125), relu_()) into a torch.cuda.CUDAGraph on a side stream during the first call for a given shape, then replaying it; (2) on any pointer mismatch or capture exception, running the same zero-allocation eager chain into torch.empty_like(x). Coefficients and op order mirror reference() exactly (x*1.25, then +y*0.75, then -0.125) so numerical divergence stays near fp32 epsilon, far inside the 1e-5 atol/rtol. Input tensors are never mutated.\",\"files\":[\"run.py\"],\"sourceReferences\":[],\"risks\":[\"If the harness supplies fresh input tensors with different data_ptr on every timed iteration, the pointer guard falls back to eager and the graph gain is lost (latency then equals the zero-allocation eager path, still correct and no worse than candidate-01)\",\"CUDA Graph capture is attempted at most twice per shape and at most 8 times globally; capture cost can land in a warmup iteration if the first call is timed\",\"The replayed path returns a persistent output buffer, so two consecutive results for the same (shape, pointer) key alias each other; values are identical for identical inputs, but a caller that retains and mutates the returned tensor across calls would observe aliasing\",\"Precision: y*0.75 via add_(alpha=) may contract to FMA, which is more accurate than the reference ordering, not less\"]}],\"recommendedCandidate\":\"candidate-01\",\"nextAction\":{\"type\":\"candidate.plan\",\"title\":\"Run candidate-02 through the shared-GPU queue\",\"reason\":\"The workspace has a real, bounded single-file diff ready; the system owns the serialized GPU queue and no local test execution is permitted.\",\"expectedOutput\":\"GPU correctness 4/4 plus primary/small benchmark p50 for candidate-02, to compare against baseline 14.336us and candidate-01 11.168us.\",\"risk\":\"medium\"},\"risks\":[\"Accept Gate target (99.9999% relative improvement) is arithmetically unreachable for a 14.3us baseline, since it would require sub-picosecond latency; the realistic objective is a genuine, measurable p50 reduction\",\"GPU graph behavior on the shared runner is unverified until the queued run returns; a capture failure degrades to the eager fallback rather than failing correctness\"]}",
                        "time": "刚刚"
                      }
